{0 Tail Workers}

A {{:https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/observability/logs/tail-workers/}Tail Worker}
receives a record of each invocation of another Worker, the producer: its event,
its console output, its exceptions and its outcome. Register {!Workero.tail} to
write one. It gets a list of {!Workero.Trace.t} values.

{1 Configure}

A tail Worker is a separate Worker. Build it as its own program, with its own
[wrangler.toml] and no routes. Do not list a Worker as its own tail consumer.
The producer names the tail Worker:

{@toml[
name = "my-app"
main = "_worker/index.js"

[[tail_consumers]]
service = "my-tail"
]}

The tail Worker has its own [name]:

{@toml[
name = "my-tail"
main = "_worker/index.js"
compatibility_date = "2026-10-01"

[build]
command = "workero build src/main.bc.wasm.js"
]}

The tail Worker must register {!Workero.tail}. A Worker without a [tail]
export that a producer lists as a consumer makes [workerd] log
[Handler does not export a tail() function] at the first event. The producer is
not affected.

{1 Handle trace items}

This tail Worker logs the exceptions of the producer:

{@ocaml[
let () =
  Workero.tail (fun traces _env _ctx ->
      List.iter
        (fun trace ->
          List.iter
            (fun (e : Workero.Trace.exn_info) ->
              Workero.Console.error (e.name ^ ": " ^ e.message))
            (Workero.Trace.exceptions trace))
        traces)
]}

The runtime calls the handler after the producer invocation ends, and for the
sub-requests that the producer makes through service bindings. A fetch item
arrives after the response body has been read or the response has been
canceled. The handler runs in a fiber, so it can call {!Workero.fetch}, a
binding and {!Workero.Promise.await}. Use {!Workero.Context.wait_until} for work
that must finish after the handler returns. An exception in the handler is not
reported to the producer: log inside the handler.

{1 Read a trace item}

- {!type:Workero.Trace.outcome} is [Ok], [Exception], [Canceled] (often a client
  that disconnected) or another {!type:Workero.Trace.outcome}.
- {!type:Workero.Trace.event} is [Fetch] with its {!Workero.Trace.Request.t} and
  response status, [Scheduled], [Queue], [Alarm], [Email], [Rpc], [Tail],
  [Hibernatable_websocket], [Custom], or [Other] with the raw value for any
  other event.
- {!Workero.Trace.logs} lists each console call with its level and
  its arguments as [Jsonkit.t] values. The runtime serializes the arguments
  before it gives them to the tail Worker, so [undefined] reads as [`Null], a
  [BigInt] as a string and a function as its source text.
- {!Workero.Trace.exceptions} lists the uncaught exceptions.
- {!Workero.Trace.script_name}, {!Workero.Trace.event_timestamp},
  {!Workero.Trace.truncated}, {!Workero.Trace.wall_time} and
  {!Workero.Trace.cpu_time} give the rest.

The URL and headers of a request are redacted by default.
{!Workero.Trace.Request.unredacted} gives the raw values. A field that has no
accessor, such as [scriptVersion], is read from {!Workero.Trace.to_js} with
{!Workero.Decode}.

{1 Stream events}

A streaming tail Worker gets each event of an invocation as it happens, not
after it ends. Register {!Workero.tail_stream}. The runtime calls it once per
invocation with the onset, and the function that it returns gets the later
events, up to the outcome.

The producer lists the Worker in [streamingTails] in a [workerd] config.

{@ocaml[
let () =
  Workero.tail_stream (fun onset _env _ctx ->
      (match Workero.Trace.Stream.kind onset with
      | Onset { info = Fetch { url; _ }; _ } -> Workero.Console.log url
      | _ -> ());
      fun event ->
        match Workero.Trace.Stream.kind event with
        | Exception { name; message; _ } ->
            Workero.Console.error (name ^ ": " ^ message)
        | _ -> ())
]}

{!Workero.Trace.Stream.kind} is {!type:Workero.Trace.Stream.kind}: [Onset],
[Log], [Exception], [Return], [Outcome], [Span_open], [Span_close],
[Diagnostic_channel], [Attributes] or [Other]. Events of one invocation share
{!Workero.Trace.Stream.invocation_id} and arrive with a growing
{!Workero.Trace.Stream.sequence}.

{1 Limits}

In local [workerd], {!Workero.Trace.wall_time} and {!Workero.Trace.cpu_time}
are [0.] and {!Workero.Trace.script_name} is [None]. The tests cannot produce
an email or a custom event, so {!Workero.Trace.Email} and
{!Workero.Trace.Custom} have no test.
